\section{Background and Problem Setup}
\label{sec:background}

We formalize compound sparsity, define the Interaction Index, and introduce phase segmentation. Full mathematical details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
Let $s \in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget. \emph{Attention head pruning} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of heads per layer by Frobenius norm importance \cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}. \emph{KV cache eviction} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of token positions from the cache. We compare two strategies: \emph{importance-based}, which scores positions by position-normalized cumulative attention mass (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance} in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}), and \emph{random}, which evicts uniformly at random \cite{nacl-acl2024}. Applying both simultaneously yields \emph{compound sparsity}; the two axes interact because head pruning alters the importance ranking used for eviction \cite{spark-aaai2026}. Existing joint-pruning approaches such as GRASPrune \cite{graspune-acl2026} learn global budget-constrained gates but treat decisions as static across generation.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.}
For a text segment, let $\text{PPL}_c$ denote perplexity under condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ (dense, attention-only, KV-only, compound). Normalizing by the dense baseline yields $r_c = \text{PPL}_c / \text{PPL}_d$, and:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{II} = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1.
    \label{eq:ii}
\end{equation}
$\mathrm{II} > 0$ indicates super-additive degradation; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive independence. We adopt the additive null ($\mathrm{II} = 0$ under no interaction) as the primary formulation. Although the two sparsity axes are causally coupled---head pruning alters the importance scores that KV eviction relies on (\S\ref{sec:analysis})---the additive null provides the most \emph{conservative} baseline: it assumes compound loss equals the sum of individual losses ($r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$), the weakest independence assumption. If even this permissive null is rejected ($\mathrm{II} > 0$), the interaction is super-additive regardless of null specification. Appendix~\ref{app:multiplicative-null} reports parallel results under the multiplicative null ($r_b = r_a \cdot r_k$), where compound effects are sub-multiplicative ($\mathrm{II}_{\times} < 0$) but the phase-severity gradient is preserved ($G_{\times}{=}21$--$64{\times}$), confirming that our conclusions are null-choice invariant. Because the II distribution is heavily right-skewed, we report medians throughout.

\paragraph{Severity gradient.}
To capture phase variation, the per-problem \emph{amplification ratio} is:
\begin{equation}
    G^{(i)} = \frac{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{high}}},
    \label{eq:gradient}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low/high}}$ is the interaction index in the low/high-sparsity phase for problem $i$. We report $G = \widetilde{G^{(i)}}$, the median across problems, with bootstrap CIs. We use the median of per-problem ratios (not the ratio of medians) to preserve per-problem pairing; see Appendix~\ref{app:profiling-subset} for comparison. $G \gg 1$ indicates phase-concentrated severity.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
\label{par:phase-segmentation}
Inspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, where explicit \texttt{<think>} markers delineate reasoning, token-level agreement between sparsity-based phases and model-generated boundaries yields Cohen's $\kappa{=}0.887$ ($n{=}48$; Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation}), indicating that the sparsity proxy captures semantically meaningful computational structure. Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

